# <%= current_page.data.title %>

New to the team, or setting up a fresh machine? Let's get you ready to contribute.

## Before you start

1. **Check your repository access:** [Integration Hub](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/integration-hub)
   holds our documentation and backlog;
   [Modernisation Platform Environments](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments)
   holds our infrastructure code. Ask in `#integration-hub-team` if you're unsure
   which repository or permissions you need.
2. **Clone it and open it** in your editor.
3. **Follow its README** to install dependencies and run the local checks.

For this documentation site, use the
[local development instructions](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/integration-hub#local-development).

## Set up the AI toolkit

APM (Agent Package Manager) installs and updates our team's engineering guidance
for GitHub Copilot.

> The toolkit currently supports GitHub Copilot only.

### 1. Install APM

Follow the [APM installation guide](https://microsoft.github.io/apm/getting-started/installation/)
then check it's available:

```sh
apm --version
```

### 2. Install the toolkit

In a terminal at the root of the
[Integration Hub repository](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/integration-hub), run:

```sh
apm install --target copilot
```

This installs the `apm.yml` dependencies, using locked versions where available,
and puts the guidance in `.github/instructions/`.

> After installation, you should see the toolkit's instruction files in
> `.github/instructions/`. Take a look in your editor's file tree, or run
> `ls .github/instructions/` in your terminal.

## Keep it up to date

From the same repository root, run this **before starting work** and **before
opening or updating a pull request**, even without AI:

```sh
apm update
```

Review and confirm APM's update plan. This updates the toolkit, not APM itself.

Read any changed guidance, check your work against it and, if using Copilot,
make sure it loads it.

| File or folder | What to do |
| --- | --- |
| `apm.yml` and `apm.lock.yaml` | Review and commit any changes. |
| `.github/instructions/` and `apm_modules/` | Generated files: don't edit or commit. |

You're welcome to submit a pull request to our toolkit here:
[Integration Hub engineering toolkit](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/ai-toolkit/tree/main/toolkits/integration-hub/engineering).

## Working outside the Integration Hub repository

We also work in repositories such as [Modernisation Platform Environments](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments).
But the setup above stays in your Integration Hub checkout, so it won't follow you
when you switch repositories.

To take the guidance with you, install the toolkit for your user account.
You only need to do this once on each machine, and you don't need to add files
to the other repositories you work in.

### Set it up once

With APM installed, you can run this from any directory:

```sh
apm install --global --target copilot ministryofjustice/ai-toolkit/toolkits/integration-hub/engineering
```

### Keep this copy up to date

Before starting work and before opening or updating a pull request, run this
from any directory:

```sh
apm update --global --target copilot
```

Review and confirm the update plan, then read what's changed.

### Make sure Copilot can see it

Copilot CLI looks for your guidance in `~/.copilot/instructions/`. Current
versions of VS Code can use it too, but the setup depends on which agent runs
your chat (called the *harness* in VS Code):

- **Copilot harness:** the installation above puts the files where it expects them.
- **Local harness:** add `~/.copilot/instructions` as an enabled folder in
  `chat.instructionsFilesLocations` in your **User settings**.

After installing or updating, open the repository you're working in and start
a fresh chat. It's worth checking the guidance is being picked up:

- **In VS Code**, look for the toolkit instructions in the Agent Customizations
  editor. Try a small, relevant task and expand **References** in the response
  to see which instructions were used.
- **In Copilot CLI**, ask Copilot to read the relevant installed instruction
  file before starting the task, and check that it actually reads it.

## Before opening a pull request

- **Refresh the toolkit.**
- **Review all AI-generated changes.**
- **Run the relevant checks:** [documentation checks](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/integration-hub#local-development)
  or [file-transfer tests](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/integration-hub/blob/main/tests/file-transfer/README.md).
  Follow the test guide's access requirements before running live AWS tests.
- **Explain what changed, why and how you checked it**, including checks you didn't run.

> **Keep sensitive data out of prompts, logs and pull requests. Never include secrets.**
